WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:04.837
Across UX Labs studies, maps had a 68% click rate.

2
00:00:05.187 --> 00:00:08.047
Travelers plan on maps. They check what's close,

3
00:00:08.047 --> 00:00:10.192
and whether a day is even possible.

4
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:18.489
On Bing,

5
00:00:18.489 --> 00:00:22.583
planning a trip meant switching between apps and sites for hotels,

6
00:00:22.583 --> 00:00:24.599
flights, activities and transit.

7
00:00:24.949 --> 00:00:27.753
There was no way to narrow the map to what mattered,

8
00:00:27.753 --> 00:00:30.180
and little help deciding where to go at all.

9
00:00:32.267 --> 00:00:33.988
Travelers don't plan in lists.

10
00:00:34.338 --> 00:00:35.661
They plan in places.

11
00:00:38.067 --> 00:00:40.584
Many travelers start without a destination,

12
00:00:40.584 --> 00:00:42.281
so the map starts there too.

13
00:00:42.631 --> 00:00:44.511
It groups places by continent,

14
00:00:44.511 --> 00:00:47.957
offers trips nearby priced from where you're browsing,

15
00:00:47.957 --> 00:00:51.403
and themes like beaches or adventure narrow the field.

16
00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:56.942
Then I brought every travel vertical onto a single map.

17
00:00:57.292 --> 00:00:59.307
One search covers destinations,

18
00:00:59.307 --> 00:01:02.816
hotels and attractions, and the map pins all of them,

19
00:01:02.816 --> 00:01:06.520
so a hotel's distance from the sights shows at a glance.

20
00:01:08.733 --> 00:01:12.320
But a map with every pin is as hard to read as a long list.

21
00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:16.433
So filters narrow it to attractions, stays or restaurants,

22
00:01:16.433 --> 00:01:18.408
where a heat map shows demand,

23
00:01:18.408 --> 00:01:20.845
and transit shows how to get around.

24
00:01:23.233 --> 00:01:29.323
I explored the map from mild to wild. The mild ideas gave engineering something to build early,

25
00:01:29.323 --> 00:01:32.144
and the wild ones showed where it could go.

26
00:01:32.494 --> 00:01:35.129
Then I tested the concepts with users,

27
00:01:35.129 --> 00:01:38.499
prioritized an MVP with product and engineering,

28
00:01:38.499 --> 00:01:40.676
and delivered its key modules.

29
00:01:43.567 --> 00:01:45.567
Design without constraints first,

30
00:01:45.567 --> 00:01:47.810
then let the team decide what ships.
